# Rust
/router/target/
**/*.rs.bk

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/

# Model caches and training artifacts.
#
# The bge-small ONNX model is ~130MB and is fetched on first run into the cache
# directory shared with zeromem; it is never committed. `weights.json` IS
# committed, being 384 floats plus a bias that the router cannot route without,
# but the intermediate embedding matrices used to fit it are not.
/train/.cache/
/train/embeddings-*.npy
*.onnx

# Runtime state
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
/deploy/.state/

# Secrets. The stack needs exactly one credential (NEURALWATT_API_KEY); it is
# supplied by the environment or a systemd EnvironmentFile, never by a tracked
# file. `.env.example` is tracked and documents the variable names.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Editor and OS
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# The training set, downloaded by train/fetch_dataset.py. ~50MB, and
# reproducible from a one-line command, so it is fetched rather than tracked.
/train/prompts.jsonl

# The routing core, compiled for iOS. 84 MB of build output, reproducible from
# the Rust beside it by scripts/build-ios-core.sh, and rebuilt on every change to
# the core, so a repository carrying it would carry those bytes again each time.
ios/WattRouterFFI.xcframework/
ios/.build/

# The Android core, built by `just android-core` from the same crate the iOS
# xcframework comes from. Build output on the same terms, and a 4 MB binary is
# not something to track.
android/core/src/main/jniLibs/

# Gradle's own output and the SDK path it wants written down. local.properties is
# machine-specific and is exactly the file somebody commits by accident.
#
# `*/build/` rather than a line per module: the root project has one too, and a
# third module would otherwise arrive with its output tracked until somebody
# noticed.
android/*/build/
android/build/
android/.gradle/
android/local.properties

# The Kotlin compiler daemon's session markers, which none of the lines above
# match: `android/*/build/` is a module's output and this sits beside `.gradle`
# rather than under a module. They are empty files naming a daemon that has
# exited, so a `git add -A` during a build tracks two names that mean nothing
# afterwards, and a checkout of a branch carrying them writes them back.
android/.kotlin/

# The Xcode project, generated from ios/project.yml by `just ios-project`. A
# checked-in pbxproj is unreviewable under this repository's size limit: it is
# generated, it reorders itself between Xcode versions, and it conflicts on every
# parallel change. The spec beside it says the same thing in a file people can read.
ios/WattRouter.xcodeproj/

# The app's Info.plist, written by `just ios-project` from the `info:` block in
# ios/project.yml. Tracking it would be a second copy of those keys, and the
# generator overwrites it, so the copy would lose every regeneration.
ios/App/Info.plist
ios/Share/Info.plist

# The entitlements, on the same terms: written from the `entitlements:` blocks in
# ios/project.yml, which is where the App Group identifier is stated once for
# both targets.
ios/App/WattRouter.entitlements
ios/Share/WattRouterShare.entitlements

# Heap dumps. R8 writes one when it runs out of memory, which is how
# android/gradle.properties came to exist.
*.hprof

# Written by scripts/stub-model.py: every request the app made while it was
# pointed at the stub. A transcript of somebody testing, so not tracked.
stub-requests.jsonl
